WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 82: The Great Charleston Earthquake of 1886: The Night the Lowcountry Shook

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:16.240
[Rebecca] On the warm, humid evening of August 31, 1886, the historic city of Charleston, South Carolina was settling into quiet nighttime slumber.

4
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:23.280
[Rebecca] Gas lamps flickered along cobblestone streets, and horse-drawn carriages clattered past stately antebellum mansions.

5
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:30.320
[Rebecca] Then, at 9 51 p.m., an ominous roar like a hundred freight trains approached from beneath the earth.

6
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:41.840
[Rebecca] For sixty terrifying seconds, the ground convulsed violently in a magnitude 7.3 earthquake, the most powerful seismic event ever recorded in the eastern United States.

7
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:50.880
[Rebecca] Historic church steeples snapped, 2,000 brick buildings collapsed into mountains of rubble, and geysers of hot mud erupted from the low country swamps.

8
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:58.320
[Rebecca] George brings us the dramatic, harrowing story of the Great Charleston earthquake.

9
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:15.680
[George] Nestled upon a narrow peninsula between the Ashley and Cooper Rivers, Charleston had endured British naval bombardments during the American Revolution.

10
00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:24.880
[George] Slave insurrections, and the longest siege in modern military history during the Civil War, which had left wide swathes of the city in charred ruins.

11
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:30.400
[George] But by 1886, the city was staging a proud cultural and commercial renaissance.

12
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:45.680
[George] Its picturesque avenues were lined with iconic, single houses boasting tiered wooden piazzas designed to catch sea breezes, grand neoclassical banks with Corinthian marble columns and towering historic church spires, none more beloved than St.

13
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:51.760
[George] Michael's Episcopal Church, whose historic bells had chimed the hours since 1764.

14
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.800
[George] But Charleston's picturesque beauty rested upon a fatal geological foundation.

15
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:12.520
[George] Centuries of artificial land reclamation had expanded the peninsula using soft marsh mud, sawdust, cotton bales, and loose harbor fill, creating an unstable, water-saturated sponge primed for catastrophic seismic liquefaction.

16
00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:27.160
[George] Architects had built Charleston with thick masonry walls and ornate cast-iron balconies to withstand coastal Atlantic hurricanes, unaware that heavy brick construction without steel reinforcement was exceptionally vulnerable to seismic shockwaves.

17
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:40.440
[George] Charleston wealthy merchants had rebuilt the city after the Civil War with pride and determination, constructing grand cotton exchanges, shipping offices, and bank headquarters along the bustling Cooper River waterfront.

18
00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:53.160
[George] Local historians noted that the historic city had survived earthquakes in 1757 and 1811, leading city engineers to assume that the lowcountry geology was immune to severe seismic trauma.

19
00:02:53.160 --> 00:03:00.360
[George] Tuesday, August 31, 1886, was a sultry, stifling late summer evening.

20
00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:16.520
[George] At 9:51 p.m., as families prepared for bed and patrons conversed in hotel lobbies, a strange, subterranean rumble was heard in the distance, resembling the sound of heavy artillery wagons rolling across iron bridges or distant thunderstorm squalls.

21
00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:24.280
[George] Within four seconds, the low hum became a deafening, terrifying roar that seemed to rise directly from the bedrock beneath the city.

22
00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:32.120
[George] The ground rose and fell in undulating waves two feet high, tossing pedestrians into the gutters and knocking horses from their feet.

23
00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:42.280
[George] Heavy mahogany furniture slid across living room floors, grand pianos leaped into the air, and plaster rained from ceilings in blinding, choking clouds.

24
00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:57.440
[George] For sixty agonizing seconds, the earth convulsed with concussive vertical thrusts and violent horizontal twists, transforming a peaceful southern city into a swirling vortex of pulverized masonry, shattered glass, and human screams.

25
00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:10.080
[George] The sound was described by survivors as an unearthly roar resembling hundreds of locomotives screaming through subterranean caverns, followed instantly by violent vertical shocks that threw adults off their feet.

26
00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:22.400
[George] Horses bolted in blind terror through the dusty streets, carriages were overturned by rolling seismic swells, and the night air was filled with the deafening sound of falling chimneys and crumbling brickwork.

27
00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:32.080
[George] The sulfuric odor rising from broken ground was so intense that residents believed a subterranean coal mine or oil pocket had ignited beneath the peninsula.

28
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.240
[George] The physical destruction wrought in that single minute was catastrophic.

29
00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:43.600
[George] Two thousand brick buildings, nearly every masonry structure in the city were severely damaged or completely flattened.

30
00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:55.520
[George] Massive stone cornices, chimney stacks, and decorative parapets detached from three-story facades, crashing down through wooden roofs and showering sidewalks in avalanches of red brick.

31
00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:56.160
[George] At St.

32
00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:03.440
[George] Michael's Church, the massive stone steeple sank eight inches into the foundation, cracking the ancient nave walls.

33
00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:09.920
[George] The medical college of South Carolina disintegrated into rubble, burying medical equipment and specimens.

34
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:20.160
[George] Gas mains ruptured beneath buckling cobblestone avenues, sparking dozens of terrifying structural fires that threw crimson shadows against the billowing dust clouds.

35
00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:32.040
[George] Over 100 residents were killed instantly, crushed beneath falling brick walls in their beds or struck down while fleeing into the streets, while hundreds more suffered severe fractures and lacerations.

36
00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:45.480
[George] Rescuers worked by lantern light through the night, clawing through heaps of fallen brick to pull buried victims from the wreckage, while volunteer bucket brigades fought gas fires that threatened entire residential blocks.

37
00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:57.240
[George] The municipal hospital was evacuated onto the lawn as masonry walls collapsed, with doctors and nurses performing emergency operations by the light of kerosene lanterns under the open night sky.

38
00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:07.960
[George] The rubble blocked fire engine horses and hose carts, forcing citizens to pull burning timbers apart with bare hands to prevent flames from spreading to wooden residential rows.

39
00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:17.880
[George] Terrified that aftershocks would bring surviving walls crashing down upon them, Charleston's entire population of 60,000 people fled into the open streets.

40
00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:30.520
[George] In the darkness, illuminated only by burning buildings and flickering lanterns, citizens gathered in open parks, churchyards, and along the wide stone promenade of White Point Garden at the battery.

41
00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:36.360
[George] Social distinctions, racial divisions, and economic hierarchies vanished in the shared terror.

42
00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:44.920
[George] Wealthy aristocrats in nightgowns huddled beside impoverished African American laborers, sharing blankets and comforting screaming children.

43
00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:57.080
[George] Ministers of every denomination led soaring prayer circles in the parks, while thousands joined in singing old spirituals and hymns beneath the starlit sky, convinced that the end of the world had arrived.

44
00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:11.000
[George] Rumors spread that the entire peninsula was sinking into the ocean, prompting frantic families to camp on the wet grass for days, refusing to enter any standing structure even as drenching tropical thunderstorms moved in from the sea.

45
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:22.560
[George] Rich and poor alike slept on wet mattresses in Washington Square and Marion Square, singing hymns by candlelight as tremors continued throughout the night, shaking the ground every few hours.

46
00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:34.000
[George] Campfires illuminated the green lawns of the public parks, where families huddled under patchwork quilts, cooking whatever rations could be salvaged from the ruined kitchens of their damaged homes.

47
00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:45.040
[George] In Marion Square, over 5,000 people gathered around the Citadel cadet barracks, praying in unison as the earth trembled with repeated rumbling aftershocks every half hour.

48
00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:54.960
[George] While Charleston suffered the greatest structural damage, the epicenter of the earthquake lay twenty miles to the northwest, near the pine forested village of Somerville.

49
00:07:54.960 --> 00:08:03.520
[George] There, the seismic energy was so violent that railroad tracks were twisted into serpentine escurves and ties were splintered like toothpicks.

50
00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:10.320
[George] The intense ground shaking generated a terrifying geological phenomenon known as seismic liquefaction.

51
00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:18.080
[George] Underground water tables were compressed with immense hydraulic pressure, forcing underground aquifers to erupt through fissures in the earth.

52
00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:30.080
[George] Hundreds of sand craters and mud geysers burst through cotton fields and forests, shooting jets of hot, sulphurous water, blue clay, and white sand 15 to 20 feet into the air.

53
00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:40.400
[George] Rural farmers watched in disbelief as fissures four feet wide opened and closed in the earth, swallowing livestock and venting noxious gases that smelled of rotten eggs.

54
00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:51.840
[George] Geologists later estimated the earthquake's magnitude at 7.3, noting that it was felt across two million square miles, from Boston and Chicago to New Orleans and Cuba.

55
00:08:51.840 --> 00:09:04.360
[George] Geologists who traveled to the epicenter were astonished by the immense crater pits, some measuring 20 feet across, which had spewed thousands of tons of wet sand and sulfuric water onto surrounding farmland.

56
00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:16.440
[George] Scientific investigators from across the nation documented hundreds of boiling sand craters, marveling at the incredible underground pressures that had forced deep aquifers to erupt through solid earth.

57
00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:27.160
[George] The railroad lines between Charleston and Savannah were severed in multiple locations, leaving the city isolated from national telegraph and rail communication for two days.

58
00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:37.640
[George] In the weeks following the disaster, the resilient people of Charleston undertook an extraordinary reconstruction campaign that permanently altered the architectural character of the city.

59
00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:42.840
[George] To stabilize thousands of fractured brick facades without demolishing historic homes.

60
00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:46.040
[George] Innovative builders developed earthquake bolts.

61
00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:59.800
[George] Long iron tie rods were threaded entirely through the interior timber floor joists of homes, anchored to the exterior brick walls by heavy decorative cast iron plates shaped like stars, crosses, discs, and S-srolls.

62
00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:11.320
[George] When tightened with turnbuckles, the bolts pulled the exterior masonry walls tightly against the interior wooden framing, creating an integrated, seismically resistant structural cage.

63
00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:25.720
[George] Today, thousands of these decorative iron earthquake washers are visible on historic homes throughout Charleston's historic district, standing as handsome architectural badges of honor and tangible reminders of the night, the city refused to fall.

64
00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:38.280
[George] The widespread installation of earthquake bolts not only stabilized the historic structures, but inadvertently preserved hundreds of 18th century homes that survived into the modern era as architectural treasures.

65
00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:51.520
[George] The cast iron earthquake bolts became an iconic symbol of Charleston indomitable resilience, demonstrating how engineering ingenuity and architectural beauty could unite to protect a historic city from future destruction.

66
00:10:51.520 --> 00:11:04.480
[George] Local blacksmiths worked day and night hammering hot iron bars into ornamental tie rods, creating a distinct Charleston architectural tradition that blended structural safety with classical decorative ironwork.

67
00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:11.360
[George] The 1886 Charleston earthquake remains one of the greatest scientific enigmas in modern seismology.

68
00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:25.840
[George] Unlike California or Japan, which sit on active tectonic plate boundaries where faults are visible and well understood, Charleston lies in the passive interior of the North American plate, thousands of miles from the nearest plate boundary.

69
00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:41.200
[George] Scientists eventually identified the Middleton Place Somerville seismic zone, a hidden system of ancient faults buried three miles beneath the coastal plain sediments, where ancient stresses within the continental crust occasionally release immense seismic energy.

70
00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:53.120
[George] The catastrophe spurred the founding of modern seismological monitoring in the eastern United States and inspired the United States Geological Survey's first comprehensive earthquake investigations.

71
00:11:53.120 --> 00:12:09.600
[George] Today, as carriages glide peacefully past Charleston's pastel mansions and church bells ring across the harbor, the hidden fault lines sleep beneath the lowcountry soil, a silent reminder that nature's most terrifying power can awaken in the most unexpected corners of the Earth.

72
00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:23.200
[George] Modern seismologists continue to study the Charleston fault system, utilizing advanced satellite radar and deep borehole sensors to understand the mysterious tectonic forces that operate in the interior of stable continents.

73
00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:34.920
[George] The memory of the great Charleston earthquake reminds modern geologists that the calm interior of the North American continent harbors immense seismic forces capable of awakening with catastrophic power.

74
00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:46.280
[George] The Charleston earthquake spurred Congress to fund the nation's first systematic geological earthquake mapping, establishing the scientific foundation for modern American seismology.

75
00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:54.920
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

76
00:12:54.920 --> 00:13:02.040
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we board a Confederate warship in Charleston Harbor for the daring escape of Robert Smalls.

